Conclusions
The basis of astrology's revolutionary development into a science consists of this: in any well handled horoscope, there is a chain of results which progressively multiply the factor counting against coincidence; one then attains near-certainty.
In the analysis of past events as well as forecasts, directions must be confirmed to the highest degree by precessed Lunars and Transits.
Some astrologers obviously err in their approach to astrology.
They regard astrology as an art, neglecting the fact that
a) Astrology --if not mutilated and ..swollen by fictional factors which cannot be verified--is already a science in an empirical sense; for there ne11er has been any doubt about the symbolism of the planets, the efficacy of angles, etc.
b) Astrology becomes a real science through measurements which in most
cases obviate coincidence.
Astrology has been considered an art by some astrologers because no visible progress has been made in this science for centuries.
  I agree, however, that up to the development of Primary directions in the Topocentric system, with the revolutionary finding of the Conception horoscope and the Dual-Test, it was not possible to verify the power of astrological directional factors.
 This is already a first step towards cautious, modest prediction. As I have reiterated, forecasts of events are amply justified and are likely to succeed if not only the horoscope of the native, but also those of his relatives and friends, have been properly studied.

