A Philosophy Revitalized

Since the birth of humankind people of all races have pondered the meaning of life, creating in the process all manner of philosophies and religions. lhose that took root have in turn sprouted countless sects, different ways to worship God.

For many years I believed it was only possible to determine the outcome of events from dates fixed beforehand, or on a speculative basis, such as the death of old or fatally ill people. Meanwhile revolutionary developments in astrology have reached a stage where logically we must seriously recon  sider the notion of predetermination, or what we calI fate.

There is plenty of evidence of accurate prophecy. The following prediction by Nostradamus in his Centurions speaks for itself:

Hitler will be desiuely defeated hy the Red Emperor of Armenia near Coelln.

" The Red Emperor of Armenia " surely refers to Stalin who was of Georgian origin. In Nostradamus  day Coelln was a smal village, now incorporated as Neukolln in the suburbs of Berl in, which did not then exist. Any accurate prophecy implies of course that all events preceeding its fulfilment are somehow programmed to occur in their inevitable order.

The revolutionairy developments in astrology mentioned above now provide cogent and abundant evidence that major events can be traced through characteristic primary and secondary directions, supported by other proven astrological systems. My books offer numerous examples of major events in the lives of natives, such as births, marriages and death, prefigured in rectified horoscopes.

Another most evident phenomen is the fact that places fortunate or unfortunate for the native, in the astrological sense, are constantly the scene of respectively fortunate or unfortunate events in his or her life.
For instance Boris Becker has always enjoyed luck when playing lawn tennis at Wimbledon, winning three championships. 8y contrast Ivan Lendl, No. I seed for 270 weeks, has never won the annual Wimbledon championship in ten successive bids.

Any experienced astrologer knows that the actual place of residence frequently decides the native s fortune or misfortune. Yet clearly the native s fortune and misfortune do not simply take place in space.

They also take their cue from appropriate astrological indications, mainly transits and cycles at the time of the event. This" duplicity of happenings" witnessed in the analysis of many nativities, defies the mathematical laws of probability. Hence we are not dealing here with simple coinci  dence.

Destiny, as evidenced by a host of accurate predictions, bears an indubitable relation to what we call fate. May i humbly beg the reader to give serious consideration to this issue, which has preoccupied so many of our forebears down the years, though within the narrow confines of our three- dimensinal space we cannot by our nature free ourselves of a sense of responsibility for our actions.

